  <h2 id="situation">Which situation is actually yours</h2>

  <p>
    "What can I actually do with PCB and no Maths?" usually turns out to be three different questions bundled into one worry.
  </p>

  <p>
    One version is: which exams and degrees are simply closed to me. Another is: which of the "hard" exams are secretly fine, just uncomfortable. A third is: is there any real route into data-and-numbers work that does not run through a Maths-mandatory engineering degree.
  </p>

  <p>
    All three get answered below, in that order, so you can find your actual situation instead of reading a generic list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-changes">What "no Maths" really changes</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the fact that resolves most of the anxiety in this decision.
  </p>

  <p>
    A missing Mathematics subject on your Class 12 marksheet blocks a short, specific list of eligibility rules — not your career in general. Those rules live at three levels: the direct-entry engineering and architecture route (JEE Main, NATA), a handful of named degrees like Economics Honours at some universities, and one or two named exam posts like SSC CGL's Junior Statistical Officer role.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything else either never asked for Maths in the first place, or asks for a quantitative-aptitude skill that any bachelor's-degree holder is expected to build through practice — not a Class 12 subject you were supposed to have already banked.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The usual bad advice</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"Without Maths, half your career options are gone" — vague, and mostly wrong once you check the actual eligibility pages one by one.</li>
      <li>"CAT and banking exams need Maths, so forget them" — they need a quantitative-aptitude score, not a 12th Maths subject on your marksheet.</li>
      <li>"Data careers are only for Maths-stream students" — true for the JEE-linked engineering route, false for several genuine bridge programmes.</li>
      <li>"Law, humanities, and government jobs are only for students who couldn't do Maths" — a status myth, not an eligibility fact.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of this tells you which specific doors are actually closed and which are open with a bit of quant practice — which is the only part that should drive your decision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="hard-blocked">Hard-blocked without Maths</h2>

  <p>
    Start here, because this is the short list that genuinely deserves the word "blocked."
  </p>

  <div class="door-grid">
