  </p>

  <h2 id="quant-heavy">Open on paper, quant-heavy in practice</h2>

  <p>
    This is the group that causes the most needless panic, because it gets lumped in with the hard-blocked list when it does not belong there.
  </p>

  <p>
    CAT, the bank PO/clerk exams, and SSC CGL never ask about your Class 12 stream. They ask for a qualifying degree, full stop. What they do ask, on the day, is a real, timed quantitative-aptitude section — and that is a different problem with a different fix.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    {quantHeavyCards.map((card) => (
      <div class:list={['compare-card', `compare-card--${card.tone}`]}>
        <div class="compare-card__label">{card.title}</div>
        <ul>
          {card.items.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A section that is a third of the paper, like CAT's Quantitative Ability, genuinely needs weekly practice over months, not a last-week cram. A section that is a quarter of a much larger paper, like SSC CGL Tier 1's Quantitative Aptitude, is a manageable, learnable gap for anyone willing to work through Class 9-10 concepts consistently.
    </p>
    <p>
      Neither is a wall. Both reward the same thing: real practice, not a Maths-stream badge you happened not to collect at 16.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    A safer way to attempt any of these: keep one fully open path — CLAT, a generalist government exam, or your existing PCB-based degree route — running in parallel while you test a quant-heavy exam. That is not indecision. It is protecting your timeline while you find out, through one real practice attempt, whether the quant section is actually a problem for you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fully-open">Fully open without Maths</h2>

  <p>
    This is the largest bucket, and it deserves more attention than the "blocked" list usually gets, because most students spend more worry on what closed than energy on what is already wide open.
  </p>

  <div class="situation-grid">
    {fullyOpenCards.map((item) => (
