        <div class="situation-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="situation-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="law-clat">Law through CLAT deserves its own honest look</h2>

  <p>
    Law is one of the most consistently under-considered options for PCB students without Maths, mostly because the myth that "science students don't do law" is louder than the actual eligibility rule.
  </p>

  <p>
    CLAT has no stream restriction whatsoever. Its five sections — English Language, Current Affairs, Legal Reasoning, Logical Reasoning, and Quantitative Techniques — are open to PCB, PCM, Commerce, and Humanities students on exactly the same terms.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Quantitative Techniques section is the part that scares people off unnecessarily. It is passage-based, tests basic data interpretation — ratios, percentages, simple charts — and is pitched around Class 10 difficulty, not an engineering-entrance level.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A PCB student who is genuinely interested in argument, structure, and applied reasoning often does better in CLAT's Legal and Logical Reasoning sections than a Commerce or Humanities peer who has never trained analytical thinking through science practicals. Do not rule this out on a stream stereotype.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="humanities-creative">Humanities-adjacent and creative pivots</h2>

  <p>
    A biology-heavy Class 12 does not lock you into biology-heavy work. Several genuinely strong pivots exist that have nothing to do with Mathematics at any stage.
  </p>

  <p>
    Psychology, mass communication and journalism, most portfolio-based design programmes, content and brand communication roles, human resources, and teaching after any bachelor's degree all run on interest, aptitude, and demonstrated work — not a Class 12 Maths subject.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    Design is the one to verify carefully, not assume. Most design entrance tests screen on visual aptitude and design thinking through their own papers, but a small number of technical-design or product-design courses lean on JEE-linked eligibility. Check the specific institute's current page before ruling a course in or out.
  </p>

  <h2 id="government-generalist">Generalist government exams stay open almost everywhere</h2>

  <p>
    UPSC Civil Services and most state public service commissions ask for one thing at the entry gate: a bachelor's degree in any discipline. Your Class 12 stream, and whether Maths appeared on it, plays no role.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your Mains optional subject is a genuinely free choice among roughly 26 options — Mathematics is one of them, never a requirement. Many toppers choose subjects like Sociology, Anthropology, or Public Administration regardless of their science or engineering background.
  </p>

  <p>
    SSC CGL, SSC CHSL, and SSC MTS follow the same broad pattern for the large majority of posts: your qualifying degree or 12th pass gets you in the door. The Junior Statistical Officer post under SSC CGL is the one specific exception, asking for 60% marks in 12th Maths or a Statistics degree.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      A useful reframe here: government exams are almost never blocked by a missing Class 12 subject.
    </p>
    <p>
      What decides your odds is selection competition, preparation time, and a parallel skill or income plan that survives an unsuccessful attempt — not whether you studied Maths at 17.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bridge-routes">The honest middle ground: bridge and foundation-Maths routes</h2>

  <p>
    Between "hard-blocked" and "fully open" sits a real, honest third category — data-adjacent work that does not need JEE-level Maths, but is not free of Maths either.
  </p>

  <ul>
    {bridgeFacts.map((item) => (
      <li>{item}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A bridge route is a real door, not a loophole. It usually asks for genuine weekly effort to rebuild Class 10-12 Maths comfort, plus a qualifying test that filters seriously. Go in expecting real work, and you will not be blindsided by the filter.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    <strong>Building a high-value skill portfolio matters more here than any single course label.</strong> A PCB student who pairs one bridge-Maths stretch with a real practice project — a small dataset summary, a basic dashboard — usually moves faster toward earlier financial freedom than one who collects course certificates without ever testing the actual work.
  </p>

